hi @jhedberg,
the cy8cproto_062_4343w board does not work ('west build -b cy8cproto_062_4343w samples\bluetooth\peripheral_dis')

i do not see CONFIG_DT_HAS_ZEPHYR_BT_HCI_UART_ENABLED in .config,

in boards\infineon\cy8cproto_062_4343w\cy8cproto_062_4343w.dts we use compatible  `infineon,cyw43xxx-bt-hci`, which actually is extension of H4. 
```
	bt-hci {
		status = "okay";
		compatible = "infineon,cyw43xxx-bt-hci";
		bt-reg-on-gpios = <&gpio_prt3 4 (GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH)>;

		/* Configuration UART speeds for firmware download (fw-download-speed) and
		 * HCI operation (hci-operation-speed).
		 * If hci-operation-speed or fw-download-speed are not defined in bt-hci{...}
		 * node, cyw43xx driver will use bus/current-speed as default speed.
		 */
		fw-download-speed = <3000000>;
	};

```
Should we update infineon,cyw43xxx-bt-hci binding or board dtsi to addition enable zephyr,bt-hci-uart as well.

Regards,
Nazar

issue_context.body:
**Is your enhancement proposal related to a problem? Please describe.**

The current Zephyr HCI driver API is a bit of an oddball, in that it portrays itself as a driver API, yet isn't one in the traditional Zephyr sense where you have driver callbacks, DTS node instances etc. Lack of DTS nodes means that we don't have a natural way of associating together the HCI driver and the physical transport it's using (e.g. it'd make sense to have a phandle property for the driver to reference a UART or SPI controller, or whatever physical device is used as the transport.

**Current API (`include/zephyr/drivers/bluetooth/hci_driver.h`)**

Host -> Controller (simplified, this would become the `__subsystem`):
```c
struct bt_hci_driver {
	const char *name;
	enum bt_hci_driver_bus bus;
	uint32_t quirks;

	int (*open)(void);
	int (*close)(void);
	int (*send)(struct net_buf *buf);

#if defined(CONFIG_BT_HCI_SETUP) || defined(__DOXYGEN__)
	int (*setup)(const struct bt_hci_setup_params *params);
#endif /* defined(CONFIG_BT_HCI_SETUP) || defined(__DOXYGEN__)*/
};
```

Controller -> Host:
```c
int bt_recv(struct net_buf *buf);
```

**Describe the solution you'd like**

HCI drivers should become a first-class Zephyr driver API along with drivers having a DTS compat string as well as associated DTS nodes. The `bt_recv()` API might make more sense as callbacks that the host passes to the driver when it calls `drv->open()` (not possible with user space though - see the notes below).

There should be a common device tree binding file that all HCI driver bindings are derived from. There are already a whole bunch of custom bindings under `dts/bindings/bluetooth/` that need to be updated.

As for how the Bluetooth host picks a driver to use, maybe using the DTS `chosen` node convention might make sense?

The `bt_hci_driver_register()` API should likely go away, since the host will be able to select the driver using devicetree.

**Challenges, assuming the API should support user space**
 - `bt_recv()` can't be a callback
 - We can't really pass `struct net_buf` between kernel and user space, rather the data will need to be copied. This may require two separate APIs, but then that'll also add some complexity to the Bluetooth host to support both.
